# Slack-Key Broken Beat

**Ingredients:** HAWAIIAN SLACK-KEY GUITAR (KĪ HŌʻALU) + BROKEN BEAT / WEST LONDON BRUK + PREPARED WURLITZER + IPU HEKE + BASS TRUMPET + SHORT ANALOG SUB

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a night residency inside a retired harbor inspection warehouse in Honolulu, where slack-key guitarist Lehua Kealoha was testing alternate open tunings beside old cargo scales while London producer Marcus Vale programmed broken-beat drums in a former customs booth. The breakthrough came when Marcus stopped slicing Lehua’s guitar into loops and instead wrote the electronic rhythm around the difference between fretted notes and ringing open strings. In slack-key playing, bass notes can continue to resonate while melody notes move above them; that natural overlap became the structural basis of the genre. Prepared Wurlitzer, ipu heke, bass trumpet, and a restrained analog sub were added to create a rhythm system where some acoustic tones deliberately survive across drum edits and bar fractures.

Slack-Key Broken Beat is an acoustic-electronic club form built around continuity inside syncopation. Broken beat supplies the chassis: displaced kicks, swung sixteenths, ghost snares, off-grid claps, bass notes that push against the barline, and short phrases that feel unstable without losing dance momentum. Hawaiian slack-key guitar supplies the counter-principle: open tunings, thumb-led bass movement, ringing sympathetic intervals, alternating bass figures, partial chords, harmonics, and treble melody notes that can decay independently.

The guitar is recorded with one close condenser near the 12th fret, one ribbon microphone near the lower bout, and a restrained room microphone. The fret microphone captures finger noise, nail attack, harmonics, and separation between bass and treble strings. The ribbon captures wood, low-mid body, and the depth of open bass strings. The room microphone remains low so the instrument stays intimate and rhythmically exact rather than scenic or cinematic.

Players favor open-tuning drones, alternating thumb bass, partial triads, sixths, suspended seconds, octave shapes, natural harmonics, short pull-offs, muted bass notes, and treble figures that leave lower strings ringing. Long soloistic flourishes are minimized. The defining feature is selective continuity: one part of the voicing may end while another remains acoustically active.

The central compositional device is the **String Carry Map**. Each important guitar phrase is divided into ANCHOR, LIFT, CARRY, and CLEAR. ANCHOR is the thumbed or struck low note establishing the tonal floor. LIFT is the treble gesture that departs from it. CARRY is the period where one or more open strings continue ringing after the played figure has moved on. CLEAR is the moment those strings are intentionally damped. Drum, sub, keyboard, and brass events are written around these states. A kick may reinforce ANCHOR, a snare may answer LIFT, upper percussion may disappear during CARRY, and a sub note may be delayed until CLEAR.

Broken-beat drums remain dry and detailed. Kicks are short, often displaced from quarter-note expectations, with occasional doubles and missing downbeats. Snares are clipped and may land slightly late. Hats and shakers use swung sixteenth grids with frequent omissions so the guitar’s own attack pattern remains audible. Small percussion bursts can accelerate briefly, but the groove must never become a dense drum solo.

Prepared Wurlitzer provides short harmonic punctuation. Felt strips, removable paper damping, and soft contact preparation reduce selected notes to woody or bell-like attacks. The instrument favors open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, and two-note voicings that answer guitar CLEAR states. Long electric-piano pads are excluded.

Ipu heke provides the acoustic low-percussion layer. Hand strikes, heel-of-palm attacks, fingertip taps, and short rolling gestures replace selected electronic percussion events rather than doubling them. Its woody, hollow resonance works especially well where programmed low percussion is removed entirely for one beat.

Bass trumpet supplies a compact brass low-mid voice. It plays dry fundamentals, short fourths and fifths, half-valve smears, breath-led entries, and single-note answers. It is not used for fanfare or orchestral weight. It enters mainly when the guitar moves upward and leaves low-mid space open.

Analog sub remains mono, short, and rhythmically conditional. It reinforces selected ANCHOR notes or enters after CLEAR, but it should rarely overlap long open-string CARRY states. This prevents the low end from masking the very resonance the style is built around.

The defining tension is fractured machine rhythm versus acoustic harmonic continuity. The beat keeps cutting the bar into unstable pieces while open strings ignore those cuts and keep sounding across them.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 124–134 BPM, usually 4/4 with broken-beat kick displacement, swung sixteenth hats, clipped snare placement, ipu-heke substitutions, short ghost-note bursts, and occasional one-eighth-note phrase extensions.

**Harmonic Palette:** Major and minor open tunings, Dorian and Mixolydian centers, suspended 2nds, 6ths, open fifths, major 7ths used sparingly, modal thirds, pedal tones, and ringing open-string intervals. Harmony changes slowly while inner voices and bass movement create momentum.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **String Carry Map** — slack-key phrases move through ANCHOR, LIFT, CARRY, and CLEAR states; electronic events are arranged around those acoustic conditions.
2. **Carry Gap** — upper percussion is deliberately removed during selected open-string resonance windows so the guitar decay remains structurally audible.
3. **Sub After Clear** — low synth pressure often enters only after the guitarist damps the open strings.
4. **Prepared-Key Reply** — short Wurlitzer dyads answer guitar CLEAR states rather than sustaining beneath the main phrase.
5. **Ipu Exchange** — selected digital kicks, rims, or low percussion hits are removed completely and replaced by dry ipu-heke strokes.
6. **No-Loop Flattening Rule** — the guitar may be edited for form, but the core performance must preserve real fingerpicked timing, open-string overlap, and live damping decisions.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through faster thumb-bass movement, denser broken-kick displacement, brighter treble harmonics, stronger ipu-heke attacks, more frequent Wurlitzer replies, and shorter CARRY windows. Climaxes remain nimble, dry, and groove-led rather than cinematic.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Slack-Key Broken Beat formed inside a retired harbor cargo-inspection warehouse in Honolulu. The building retained analog weighing scales, faded container numbers, timber pallets, steel rollers, hanging work lamps, salt-dulled windows, cargo hooks, old inspection desks, and wide concrete bays with short, hard reflections.

Slack-key guitarist **Lehua Kealoha** had been using one side room to document how different tunings changed the duration and interaction of open strings. She was particularly interested in phrases where a bass string continued to ring after the fretting hand had moved into a new treble shape.

London producer **Marcus Vale** was working nearby on broken-beat sketches. His first version chopped Lehua’s playing into short quantized loops. The timing fit, but the guitar lost its defining continuity. Lehua played the same phrase again and deliberately let one low open string ring across a drum-machine mute. Marcus removed the hats for the duration of that resonance and delayed the next bass note until she damped the string. That became the first String Carry Map.

Keyboardist **Noa Kamekona** joined with a prepared Wurlitzer whose short damped voicings could occupy the moment after guitar resonance cleared. Percussionist **Malia Reyes** added ipu heke by replacing selected digital low-percussion events rather than decorating them. Bass-trumpet player **Evan Cole** completed the group with dry low-mid phrases that entered only when the guitar moved upward.

Their first performances were called **clearance sets**. A simple chart above the stage showed four columns labeled ANCHOR, LIFT, CARRY, CLEAR. Nothing was sensor-controlled. The musicians listened for the exact moment each open string stopped or was damped.

The visual language developed around oxidized aluminum, pale pallet wood, faded harbor blue, inspection-card white, rust orange, dark indigo clothing, muted brass, and narrow sodium work light. The unofficial rule is that no electronic layer may cover an open string merely because the next bar has begun.

