## 3. Harbor Light Stays On After the Chord Is Gone

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 124 BPM with the sparsest drums on the EP. Lehua plays widely spaced slack-key harmonics and low thumbed pedals while Marcus uses only one kick, one clipped snare, and occasional shuffled hat pairs. The first two minutes contain no analog sub.

Noa plays single prepared-Wurlitzer notes only after full CLEAR states. Malia uses isolated ipu-heke bass strokes every eight bars. Evan sustains one breath-led bass-trumpet note beneath a high guitar harmonic, then disappears before the next ANCHOR.

In the final section, Lehua gradually damps each phrase sooner, reducing resonance until the guitar becomes almost purely percussive. Marcus does the opposite and progressively lengthens the silence after each beat. The last chord is stopped completely, but a harbor work lamp hum recorded in the room continues for several seconds before the tape cuts.

